1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,520
Okay, moving to the next section, cellular injury.

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:11,420
Probably the easiest way to understand this section is to put it in the context of what we just covered.

3
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:21,280
So you'll recall that I said that a cell can be stressed, and that stress can lead to growth adaptations.

4
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:26,500
In particular, if there's an increase in stress, we would expect to see hyperplasia and hypertrophy.

5
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,960
If there was a decrease in stress, we would expect to see atrophy.

6
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:35,120
And if there is a change in stress, we would expect to see metoplasia.

7
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:43,380
Now, it is possible that the stress could be so severe that it exceeds the cell's ability to adapt.

8
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:45,580
Exceeds the cell's ability to adapt.

9
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:47,100
Now, why is that important?

10
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:55,520
Because if the stress exceeds the cell's ability to adapt, then rather than undergo a growth alteration or a growth adaptation,

11
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:57,800
the cell will actually become injured.

12
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:00,640
And that's really what this section is all about, cell injury.

13
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,100
Now, we could ask a simple question here.

14
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:07,820
What determines whether the cells of an organ are going to adapt or they're going to become injured?

15
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,240
Well, actually, there's three major factors.

16
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:12,400
The first is the type of stress.

17
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:20,000
So, for example, if a cell were to, or if an organ were to become inflamed,

18
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:27,880
it's much more likely that the inflammation would result in a growth adaptation than perhaps injury.

19
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:32,560
The severity of the stress is also important.

20
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:34,920
Just to give you a very simple example,

21
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:38,960
if you slowly cut the blood supply to the kidney,

22
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:43,540
the kidney will atrophy due to the decrease in nutrients.

23
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:49,160
So, maybe a classic example of that would be atherosclerosis of the renal artery

24
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:52,200
or fibromuscular dysplasia of the renal artery.

25
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:57,460
Either of these two pathologic processes would slowly cut the blood supply to the kidney.

26
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:03,040
And, as a result, the kidney would shrink in size because it would undergo atrophy.

27
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:08,520
So, that's slowly developing ischemia or a slow cutting off of the blood supply.

28
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:14,580
However, if there was a thrombus in the, let's say, for example, left heart,

29
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:21,840
and that thrombus was to lodge, was to break off and embolize and then lodge into the renal artery,

30
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:29,620
that could block the blood supply to such an extent that a cutoff of the blood supply to, let's say,

31
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:34,920
completely would actually result in infarction or death of the kidney parenchyma.

32
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:41,200
And so, here we see that sort of the same thing, the same theme in the sense that we cut the blood supply

33
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:48,220
in the renal artery, but a slow, progressive cutting blood supply of the renal artery

34
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:54,080
would result in atrophy of the kidney, whereas a rapid, complete cutting off of the blood supply

35
00:02:54,080 --> 00:03:00,760
of the renal artery would result in infarction and injury of the cells.

36
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:07,820
So, that would be an example of the severity determining what happens in response to a stress.

37
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:15,160
And the third factor that determines what happens in response to a stress is the type of cell that is affected.

38
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:16,860
Now, what do we mean by that?

39
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:21,560
Well, one of the high-yield principles to be familiar with, and examiners like to go after this,

40
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:25,940
is the fact that neurons are very susceptible to hypoxia.

41
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:31,900
In fact, the neuron has the ability to withstand hypoxia for only three to five minutes,

42
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:39,040
whereas skeletal muscle, which is another type of cell, actually can withstand hypoxia for very long periods of time.

43
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:47,760
And so, the type of cell affected will also determine how the organ or the cell adapts to a particular stress.

44
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:51,240
Anyway, in this particular section, we're dealing with injury.

45
00:03:51,720 --> 00:03:55,040
And so, if I just very quickly read to you what we have here on our text,

46
00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:01,280
we say that injury occurs when the stress exceeds the cell's ability to adapt.

47
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:02,500
As I've already explained.

48
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:10,760
And the likelihood of injury is going to be based on the type of stress, the severity, and the type of cell that is affected.

49
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:12,140
All right.

50
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:13,400
So, moving on.

51
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:20,540
While we're on this topic of cellular injury, we should remind ourselves that as we go through the remainder of this text,

52
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:23,000
there are going to be many common causes of injury.

53
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:26,520
For example, inflammation is a common cause of injury.

54
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:28,820
You know, you can take any example.

55
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:32,180
If a patient gets an infection, a pneumonia, that's going to injure the lung.

56
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:38,180
At the same time, there are some chronic inflammatory states, like autoimmune diseases, that would also result in injury.

57
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:42,180
Nutritional excess or deficiency can result in injury.

58
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:43,660
We're all familiar with that.

59
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:48,720
In fact, much of the disease that we see in the Western world is actually due to nutritional excess.

60
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:56,040
And much of the disease that we see in areas where poverty is prevalent is really due to nutritional deficiency.

61
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:59,340
So, these are common causes of injury as well.

62
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:03,020
Hypoxia, very classic cause of injury.

63
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:10,160
You know, all of the myocardial infarctions and ischemic stroke to the brain are all examples of hypoxia.

64
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:14,720
And we're actually going to spend some time discussing hypoxia in more detail in a few minutes.

65
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:17,180
Trauma, common cause of injury.

66
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:20,260
For example, a gunshot wound to tissue will injure the cells.

67
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:21,760
And genetic mutations.

68
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:28,820
When there are mutations and proteins are not formed or not formed properly, that can also result in cellular injury.

69
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:34,320
Now, all of these themes are going to be repeated as we go through the text.

70
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:38,940
However, what I want to really focus on for the next few minutes is hypoxia.

71
00:05:39,540 --> 00:05:42,640
Hypoxia is a nice model for studying cellular injury.

72
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:46,620
And so, we're going to focus on that over the next few minutes.

73
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:53,640
So, before we can begin our discussion of hypoxia, we really need to understand the term hypoxia.

74
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:55,440
So, let's do it this way.

75
00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:56,940
Hypo means low.

76
00:05:57,600 --> 00:05:59,760
And oxia refers to oxygen.

77
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:00,820
Oxygen.

78
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:03,220
And so, hypoxia is low oxygen.

79
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:07,320
And in particular, it's low oxygen delivery to tissues.

80
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:11,980
Now, recall that tissues are essentially dependent on oxygen.

81
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:19,040
And the reason they're dependent on oxygen is because oxygen is the final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain.

82
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:26,040
That accepting of electrons by oxygen allows for the generation of ATP.

83
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:31,320
And so, low oxygen is going to impair oxidative phosphorylation, leading to low ATP.

84
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:36,140
And low ATP, which is an essential energy source, will lead to injury.

85
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:42,720
So, why don't we just summarize it by saying low oxygen, low ATP, low ATP, cellular injury.

86
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:46,320
And that's why hypoxia causes cellular injury.

87
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:52,140
So, the next discussion would be, what are the causes of hypoxia?

88
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:58,880
And actually, there are three major, broadly speaking, there are three major causes of hypoxia.

89
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:00,320
The first is ischemia.

90
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:02,340
The second is hypoxemia.

91
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:06,120
And the third is a decreased O2 carrying capacity of the blood.

92
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:09,600
We'll actually elaborate on each of these in the next few slides.

93
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:10,900
So, let's start with ischemia.

94
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,300
Ischemia is decreased blood flow through an organ.

95
00:07:15,340 --> 00:07:19,280
Probably the easiest way to think about this is, I'll draw you an image here.

96
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:21,140
This is the artery.

97
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:24,580
And it is feeding any organ.

98
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:27,360
It doesn't matter for this particular discussion.

99
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:31,860
And this is the vein, which is draining that organ.

100
00:07:32,580 --> 00:07:38,060
Now, ischemia refers to decreased blood flow through that organ.

101
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:41,420
So, this is the way that blood would be flowing through the organ, obviously.

102
00:07:42,040 --> 00:07:44,700
And ischemia can occur in two ways.

103
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:48,660
The first way, well, actually, there's three ways, but two very common ways.

104
00:07:48,740 --> 00:07:55,160
The first way would be for there to be a block in the arterial flow into the organ.

105
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:59,000
So, if there's a block in the arterial flow, for example, atherosclerosis,

106
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:01,660
that would decrease the blood flow going through the organ,

107
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,860
which would then result in ischemia within this organ.

108
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:07,540
I think we're all very familiar with that.

109
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:09,840
It's a common principle in pathology.

110
00:08:10,860 --> 00:08:13,280
It's going to result in many disease processes.

111
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:16,740
For example, when there's atherosclerosis of the coronary artery,

112
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:20,520
that results in angina, or episodic chest pain.

113
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:23,340
But we don't need to worry about those details yet.

114
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:24,160
We'll get those later.

115
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:29,480
The idea here is that decreased atherosclerosis of the artery

116
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:32,280
would result in decreased blood flow through that organ.

117
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:35,000
I think we all have that down.

118
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:38,860
The next, the other way by which we could get ischemia,

119
00:08:39,020 --> 00:08:41,200
and perhaps this is something that we don't think about as much,

120
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:43,000
but shows up often on examinations,

121
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:46,660
is that we could decrease the flow through the vein.

122
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:49,500
And if we block this vein, for example,

123
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:53,620
then we would block the flow of blood across this bed.

124
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:57,420
And by blocking the flow of fresh blood across that bed,

125
00:08:57,420 --> 00:09:00,220
we would decrease the amount of oxygen within this organ.

126
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:04,520
And that would then result in ischemia of the organ.

127
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:07,320
Now, there are many examples in pathology of this,

128
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:08,820
but there's one very high yield,

129
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:10,680
shows up on examinations all the time,

130
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:13,420
and that is a syndrome called Bud-Kiari syndrome.

131
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:16,540
Bud-Kiari syndrome we'll actually discuss in more detail

132
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:17,500
when we cover liver.

133
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:19,080
But in Bud-Kiari syndrome,

134
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:22,420
there's a thrombosis of the hepatic vein,

135
00:09:23,140 --> 00:09:25,220
and by blocking the hepatic vein,

136
00:09:25,420 --> 00:09:27,100
blood no longer can flow through the liver,

137
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:31,420
and that results in infarction within the liver parenchyma.

138
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:32,920
It can actually cause death.

139
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:36,120
That's Bud-Kiari syndrome, very high yield.

140
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:38,740
Another high yield derivative of Bud-Kiari syndrome,

141
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:40,080
as long as we're on that topic,

142
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:42,680
is that I can ask you the question,

143
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:45,100
what's the most common cause of Bud-Kiari syndrome?

144
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:47,760
So the answer in your mind should be

145
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:49,840
that the most common cause of Bud-Kiari syndrome

146
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:51,680
is polycythemia vera.

147
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,420
Recall that polycythemia vera is actually a disorder

148
00:09:56,420 --> 00:09:59,800
in which patients have a high red blood cell count

149
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:02,720
due to overproduction of red blood cells,

150
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:04,760
and that makes the blood thick and viscous.

151
00:10:05,180 --> 00:10:07,600
And the increased thickness and viscosity of the blood

152
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:11,140
leads to a complication of thrombosis or clotting.

153
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:13,600
And one place in which thrombosis can occur

154
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:14,940
is within the hepatic vein.

155
00:10:15,100 --> 00:10:17,000
Which would then lead to Bud-Kiari syndrome.

156
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:20,040
Just as long as we're on the topic,

157
00:10:20,180 --> 00:10:23,500
another classic example of Bud-Kiari syndrome

158
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:25,240
or cause of Bud-Kiari syndrome

159
00:10:25,240 --> 00:10:27,760
would be a patient with lupus

160
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:30,940
who has something called lupus anticoagulant,

161
00:10:31,380 --> 00:10:37,860
which creates a hypercoagulable state in that patient.

162
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:41,980
So anyway, I don't want to press too hard

163
00:10:41,980 --> 00:10:43,860
on the specific examples,

164
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:44,780
but really at this point,

165
00:10:44,820 --> 00:10:47,020
I'm trying to lay down a basic principle in your mind.

166
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:49,100
We'll really have a lot of time to talk about

167
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:52,120
the common and classic examples of Bud-Kiari

168
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:53,600
when we do the liver.

169
00:10:54,380 --> 00:10:57,020
So this is one way by which ischemia can occur.

170
00:10:57,140 --> 00:10:59,920
And of course, ischemia would be a cause of hypoxia,

171
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:02,120
hypoxia, which is the discussion that we're having.

172
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:05,520
A third way by which ischemia can occur is called shock.

173
00:11:05,980 --> 00:11:10,200
Shock refers to a generalized decreased perfusion

174
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:11,920
of the vital organs.

175
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:15,360
And it can occur, for example,

176
00:11:15,460 --> 00:11:17,200
if the heart's not functioning properly,

177
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:18,620
we get a cardiogenic shock.

178
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:21,780
If there's not good blood volume in the vascular space

179
00:11:21,780 --> 00:11:23,540
because, let's say, there's a gunshot wound,

180
00:11:24,020 --> 00:11:25,660
we'll have a hypovolemic shock.

181
00:11:26,340 --> 00:11:28,360
There's also a neurogenic shock,

182
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:31,440
an anaphylactic shock, a septic shock.

183
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,860
In each of these cases, the central theme

184
00:11:34,860 --> 00:11:37,100
is that there's a decreased ability

185
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:38,500
to perfuse the vital organs.

186
00:11:38,940 --> 00:11:40,560
And of course, if blood is not flowing

187
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:41,520
to the vital organs,

188
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:43,360
that would be an example of ischemia,

189
00:11:43,620 --> 00:11:46,400
and that would result in hypoxia of the cells

190
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:48,820
or decreased oxygen going to the cells.

191
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:53,500
The second cause of hypoxia is hypoxemia.

192
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:56,960
Now, hypo, we've got that same hypo

193
00:11:56,960 --> 00:12:00,140
and then ox, oxygen, low oxygen,

194
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:02,320
but emia means in the blood.

195
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:09,180
And so hypoxemia refers to a low pressure of oxygen

196
00:12:09,180 --> 00:12:12,220
or low partial pressure of oxygen within the blood.

197
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:14,700
Very specifically, it would be defined

198
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:19,560
as a p small a O2, less than 60 millimeters of mercury,

199
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:22,300
and that would translate usually to roughly

200
00:12:22,300 --> 00:12:24,520
an SAO2 of less than 90%.

201
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:27,340
This sometimes confuses students,

202
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:29,040
the concept of hypoxemia.

203
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:31,660
And so I think that maybe we should take a step back

204
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:33,600
and review a little physiology here

205
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:37,180
in order to understand this concept a little better.

206
00:12:37,740 --> 00:12:40,520
So why don't we do it in the following way?

207
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:45,040
Okay, let's remember that there's a certain partial,

208
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:48,280
there's a certain concentration of oxygen

209
00:12:48,280 --> 00:12:50,400
present within the atmosphere,

210
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:52,140
which is called the FiO2.

211
00:12:52,540 --> 00:12:54,940
This is really the fractional percentage of oxygen

212
00:12:54,940 --> 00:12:56,500
within the atmosphere.

213
00:12:57,220 --> 00:13:00,640
And the goal of the lungs is to bring that FiO2

214
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:05,520
into the alveolar air sacs

215
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:09,180
and to create something called the P big A O2.

216
00:13:10,420 --> 00:13:14,440
So the P big A O2 is a partial pressure of oxygen

217
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:16,280
within the alveolar airspace.

218
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:20,020
Now that P A O2 squeezes oxygen,

219
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:22,720
that pressure is used to squeeze oxygen

220
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:26,160
across the interstitium and into the capillaries,

221
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:29,920
which then creates a P small a O2,

222
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:32,060
which is the partial pressure of oxygen

223
00:13:32,060 --> 00:13:34,560
within the arterioles.

224
00:13:35,280 --> 00:13:38,420
And then that P A O2, that P small a O2,

225
00:13:38,560 --> 00:13:41,280
its goal is to squeeze oxygen

226
00:13:41,280 --> 00:13:43,220
across the red blood cell membrane

227
00:13:43,220 --> 00:13:45,720
to load oxygen onto hemoglobin,

228
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:48,280
creating an SaO2.

229
00:13:48,980 --> 00:13:50,420
So this is sort of a sequence

230
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:56,420
of how oxygen is squeezed eventually into hemoglobin.

231
00:13:57,340 --> 00:14:01,300
So the FiO2 results in a PaO2, big A O2,

232
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:03,380
which is the pressure of oxygen

233
00:14:03,380 --> 00:14:04,600
within the alveolar airspace.

234
00:14:05,060 --> 00:14:12,660
That squeezes oxygen across the barrier into the blood,

235
00:14:12,940 --> 00:14:15,960
which creates a P small a O2 in the arterioles.

236
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:18,820
And then that squeezes oxygen

237
00:14:18,820 --> 00:14:21,460
across the red blood cell membrane

238
00:14:21,460 --> 00:14:23,540
into the red blood cells,

239
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:25,620
which then creates an SaO2.

240
00:14:26,440 --> 00:14:29,740
Now hypoxemia, again, as I've already told you,

241
00:14:29,740 --> 00:14:32,960
refers to a low PaO2.

242
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:35,440
So really, this number would be low.

243
00:14:35,820 --> 00:14:37,940
But you can see that what actually feeds

244
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:41,280
the P small a O2 is the FiO2 and the P big A O2.

245
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:45,940
So any change prior to this in this sequence,

246
00:14:46,020 --> 00:14:47,440
in the early phases of this sequence,

247
00:14:47,540 --> 00:14:48,980
would also decrease the PaO2.

248
00:14:49,080 --> 00:14:51,060
So let's just take some common examples.

249
00:14:51,060 --> 00:14:54,320
If a patient is at high altitude,

250
00:14:56,370 --> 00:14:57,310
all right, high altitude,

251
00:14:58,010 --> 00:14:59,770
then when they're at high altitude,

252
00:15:00,010 --> 00:15:02,250
the FiO2 is actually decreased.

253
00:15:02,730 --> 00:15:05,510
Well, the partial pressure overall of oxygen in the air is decreased.

254
00:15:05,510 --> 00:15:07,010
The overall pressure is decreased.

255
00:15:07,450 --> 00:15:08,790
The atmospheric pressure is decreased.

256
00:15:08,930 --> 00:15:12,930
And therefore, the oxygen pressure is decreased at high altitude.

257
00:15:13,470 --> 00:15:14,810
So at high altitude,

258
00:15:15,510 --> 00:15:18,210
the FiO2 would go down,

259
00:15:18,210 --> 00:15:20,090
which would decrease the P big A O2,

260
00:15:20,210 --> 00:15:22,370
which would decrease the P small a O2,

261
00:15:22,750 --> 00:15:26,990
which would then result in a state called hypoxemia.

262
00:15:27,470 --> 00:15:28,690
So that's one example.

263
00:15:29,170 --> 00:15:33,430
Another example would be to decrease the P big A O2.

264
00:15:33,950 --> 00:15:36,990
The classic way by which the P big A O2 decreases

265
00:15:36,990 --> 00:15:50,970
is anything that increases the P big A CO2 would actually decrease the P A O2.

266
00:15:50,970 --> 00:15:55,730
And that's because, recall that there's a certain pressure that can exist,

267
00:15:55,890 --> 00:15:59,130
and it's a balance between oxygen, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen,

268
00:15:59,330 --> 00:16:00,490
and nitrogen is a constant.

269
00:16:00,490 --> 00:16:06,610
So any time the P big A CO2 goes up,

270
00:16:06,630 --> 00:16:10,010
any time there's carbon dioxide building up in the lung,

271
00:16:10,430 --> 00:16:13,290
the P A O2 will always go down.

272
00:16:13,690 --> 00:16:15,270
You know, they're always opposite.

273
00:16:15,830 --> 00:16:18,850
So what would be some examples where carbon dioxide would build up in the lung,

274
00:16:18,930 --> 00:16:21,250
which would then knock down the P big A O2?

275
00:16:22,490 --> 00:16:22,970
Hypoventilation,

276
00:16:23,190 --> 00:16:26,250
where carbon dioxide gets a chance to pile up in the lung

277
00:16:26,250 --> 00:16:27,790
because it's not being blown off.

278
00:16:28,210 --> 00:16:29,030
That's one example.

279
00:16:29,030 --> 00:16:33,910
Another example which would cause this would be COPD.

280
00:16:34,550 --> 00:16:38,730
In COPD, you're very familiar with the fact that air gets trapped within the lung,

281
00:16:38,890 --> 00:16:40,810
chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.

282
00:16:41,290 --> 00:16:42,790
Air is being trapped within the lung.

283
00:16:43,230 --> 00:16:45,890
And so that would also decrease the P big A O2.

284
00:16:46,450 --> 00:16:48,530
Now, once the P big A O2 goes down,

285
00:16:48,630 --> 00:16:53,110
then automatically the P small A O2 is also going to go down,

286
00:16:53,250 --> 00:16:55,290
and that's obviously going to be hypoxemia.

287
00:16:56,130 --> 00:16:58,530
Another way by which hypoxemia can occur,

288
00:16:58,530 --> 00:17:01,410
remember that the P big A O2,

289
00:17:01,410 --> 00:17:06,650
its goal is to squeeze blood into the capillaries of the alveolar air sac.

290
00:17:06,990 --> 00:17:11,050
So let's say that we thickened the alveolar air sac

291
00:17:11,050 --> 00:17:14,810
so that this pressure, the P big A O2,

292
00:17:14,890 --> 00:17:18,210
would not be able to squeeze as much blood into the capillaries.

293
00:17:18,690 --> 00:17:21,910
An example of that would be interstitial fibrosis of the lung.

294
00:17:21,910 --> 00:17:24,770
When there's interstitial fibrosis of the lung,

295
00:17:24,890 --> 00:17:30,070
the oxygen diffusion barrier increases or thickens,

296
00:17:30,430 --> 00:17:34,490
and therefore oxygen can't be squeezed very well into the blood.

297
00:17:34,990 --> 00:17:39,070
So that would be another example of how hypoxemia might occur.

298
00:17:39,070 --> 00:17:42,970
So, again, I think if you understand the general idea here,

299
00:17:43,310 --> 00:17:47,190
it gives you a very good grasp on what we mean by hypoxemia.

300
00:17:47,630 --> 00:17:52,230
Now, obviously, any time the P small A O2 here goes down,

301
00:17:52,510 --> 00:17:54,950
that's also going to decrease the SAO2

302
00:17:54,950 --> 00:17:59,450
because this is the pressure that actually was creating the SAO2.

303
00:18:00,070 --> 00:18:02,570
And you can review your oxygen dissociation curves,

304
00:18:02,630 --> 00:18:04,110
and you'll see that to be true as well

305
00:18:04,110 --> 00:18:08,230
if you were to correlate these numbers on the oxygen dissociation curve.

306
00:18:09,150 --> 00:18:11,870
So I hope that clarifies the concept of hypoxemia.

307
00:18:12,390 --> 00:18:14,590
But the general principle that I'm really trying to get at here,

308
00:18:15,070 --> 00:18:17,750
aside from the physiology that I just mentioned,

309
00:18:18,110 --> 00:18:22,730
is hypoxemia refers to a low partial pressure of oxygen within the blood,

310
00:18:22,730 --> 00:18:25,890
and it's another cause of hypoxia,

311
00:18:26,090 --> 00:18:29,610
which is, again, the broad topic that we're talking about.

312
00:18:29,610 --> 00:18:35,370
A third way by which we could create hypoxia or by which hypoxia occurs

313
00:18:35,370 --> 00:18:40,110
is to have a decreased O2 carrying capacity within the blood.

314
00:18:40,590 --> 00:18:41,990
Now, in order to understand this,

315
00:18:42,070 --> 00:18:44,610
remember that the dissolved oxygen within the blood

316
00:18:44,610 --> 00:18:47,950
actually enters the red blood cell and then binds hemoglobin.

317
00:18:48,530 --> 00:18:53,430
And it's hemoglobin that actually has the role of carrying oxygen to the tissues

318
00:18:53,430 --> 00:18:56,630
and eventually unloading it at the level of the tissues.

319
00:18:56,630 --> 00:19:02,150
Recall from physiology that the vast majority of the oxygen content of the blood

320
00:19:02,150 --> 00:19:04,570
is actually due to oxygen-bound hemoglobin.

321
00:19:05,050 --> 00:19:07,550
And so if you knock out the red blood cells

322
00:19:07,550 --> 00:19:12,990
or if you knock out the ability of the red blood cell hemoglobin to carry oxygen,

323
00:19:13,570 --> 00:19:16,370
that will also result in hypoxia within a tissue.

324
00:19:17,970 --> 00:19:22,170
Let's just give you a few examples of that to highlight this idea.

325
00:19:22,170 --> 00:19:26,190
And because it's one of the few places we'll get to discuss this principle,

326
00:19:26,690 --> 00:19:30,830
one example of a decreased O2 carrying capacity of the blood would be anemia.

327
00:19:31,430 --> 00:19:36,190
Remember that anemia refers to a decrease in the total red blood cell mass in the body.

328
00:19:36,930 --> 00:19:39,410
And when there's a decrease in red blood cell mass,

329
00:19:39,490 --> 00:19:43,750
there's going to be a decrease in the amount of oxygen being carried in the blood.

330
00:19:44,130 --> 00:19:47,750
And therefore, if there's a decrease in the amount of oxygen being carried in the blood,

331
00:19:47,750 --> 00:19:52,750
there's obviously going to be a decrease in the amount of oxygen going to the tissues, i.e. hypoxia.

332
00:19:53,510 --> 00:20:01,050
And so one important thing to remember is that if we go back to our paradigm that we had just established,

333
00:20:01,470 --> 00:20:09,990
and if you have an FiO2, and the FiO2 is generating a P big A O2,

334
00:20:09,990 --> 00:20:14,790
and that's generating a P small A O2,

335
00:20:15,230 --> 00:20:18,690
and that's eventually going to generate an SaO2,

336
00:20:19,190 --> 00:20:22,450
what we should remember is that when we have anemia,

337
00:20:22,610 --> 00:20:26,570
there's no problem with the atmospheric oxygen.

338
00:20:26,750 --> 00:20:29,510
There's no problem with the ability to bring oxygen into the lung.

339
00:20:29,590 --> 00:20:32,950
There's no problem with the ability to squeeze oxygen into the blood.

340
00:20:33,330 --> 00:20:35,530
So the PaO2 is actually normal.

341
00:20:35,530 --> 00:20:40,350
At the same time, the SaO2 is normal as well.

342
00:20:40,630 --> 00:20:46,110
And the reason the SaO2 is normal is because the SaO2 just looks at

343
00:20:46,110 --> 00:20:51,550
what percentage of the hemoglobin is bound by oxygen.

344
00:20:52,050 --> 00:20:54,910
And so if you have, you know, if you've got, I don't know,

345
00:20:54,990 --> 00:20:58,450
let's just pretend that I've got three red blood cells in my entire body,

346
00:20:58,690 --> 00:21:02,790
and each red blood cell is carrying four molecules of oxygen.

347
00:21:02,790 --> 00:21:08,790
In that case, I'm 100% bound because each cell has the ability to carry four,

348
00:21:08,890 --> 00:21:10,770
and each cell is actually carrying four.

349
00:21:11,170 --> 00:21:13,770
Now, if I get an anemia, let's say that I get an anemia,

350
00:21:13,950 --> 00:21:16,770
and I knock out one-third of my red blood cells,

351
00:21:17,230 --> 00:21:22,270
each of my remaining blood cells is still carrying four molecules of oxygen.

352
00:21:22,410 --> 00:21:26,030
And so here I'm, again, 100% bound and 100% bound.

353
00:21:26,030 --> 00:21:31,830
So the SaO2 does not, because it looks at the percentage of bound, of oxygen,

354
00:21:32,090 --> 00:21:34,970
sorry, the percentage of oxygen molecules that are bound to hemoglobin,

355
00:21:35,430 --> 00:21:37,930
it's not going to be altered in an anemia.

356
00:21:38,210 --> 00:21:39,370
So I hope that's clear.

357
00:21:40,830 --> 00:21:46,510
Another way by which we can have a decreased O2 carrying capacity of the blood

358
00:21:46,510 --> 00:21:48,090
is carbon monoxide poisoning.

359
00:21:48,610 --> 00:21:52,510
And this is a very high-yield topic for examinations.

360
00:21:52,510 --> 00:21:55,630
And so let's briefly review this.

361
00:21:55,710 --> 00:22:01,410
The idea here is that carbon monoxide is a very avid binder of hemoglobin.

362
00:22:01,590 --> 00:22:07,850
It binds hemoglobin 100 times, with a 100 times affinity as opposed compared to oxygen.

363
00:22:08,610 --> 00:22:13,310
Now, carbon monoxide is actually an odorless, colorless gas,

364
00:22:13,710 --> 00:22:17,850
and it can sneak into the lungs, and then it can bind the hemoglobin.

365
00:22:17,850 --> 00:22:21,630
And once it binds the hemoglobin, it doesn't let the hemoglobin go,

366
00:22:21,830 --> 00:22:23,450
and therefore oxygen can't bind.

367
00:22:23,970 --> 00:22:26,650
Now, in this particular case, the PaO2 will be normal.

368
00:22:27,010 --> 00:22:29,110
Again, there's no problem with the FiO2.

369
00:22:29,310 --> 00:22:31,290
There's no problem with the PaO2.

370
00:22:31,490 --> 00:22:35,330
And there's no problem with the PaO2.

371
00:22:35,830 --> 00:22:38,830
And so therefore, the PaO2 will not be changed.

372
00:22:38,910 --> 00:22:39,930
It will be normal.

373
00:22:40,050 --> 00:22:41,890
However, the SaO2 will be decreased.

374
00:22:41,890 --> 00:22:44,510
And the idea here is that if you've got a red blood cell,

375
00:22:44,790 --> 00:22:48,030
and it normally has the ability to hold four hemoglobin molecules,

376
00:22:48,370 --> 00:22:54,290
but let's say it can only hold two because of the fact that the other two spaces are bound by carbon monoxide,

377
00:22:54,610 --> 00:23:02,190
that's going to be a reduction in the percentage of spaces that are bound by oxygen.

378
00:23:02,330 --> 00:23:04,930
And so the SaO2 will actually be decreased.

379
00:23:04,930 --> 00:23:10,690
Now, important to recognize for examination purposes and for clinical purposes,

380
00:23:11,250 --> 00:23:16,330
the common exposures that lead to carbon monoxide poisoning include smoke from fires.

381
00:23:16,610 --> 00:23:21,410
So any patient that happens to be, let's say, exposed to smoke from a house fire

382
00:23:21,410 --> 00:23:24,890
or some fire of that nature can develop carbon monoxide poisoning.

383
00:23:25,750 --> 00:23:30,030
Another important source of carbon monoxide poisoning would be exhaust from cars.

384
00:23:30,030 --> 00:23:35,410
And this is the classic example of a patient who might try to commit suicide by carbon monoxide poisoning,

385
00:23:35,610 --> 00:23:38,490
where they start their car and they shut the garage door,

386
00:23:38,870 --> 00:23:42,190
and the carbon monoxide slowly builds up within that closed space.

387
00:23:42,910 --> 00:23:48,410
And then a third way by which carbon monoxide poisoning can occur is via gas heaters.

388
00:23:48,510 --> 00:23:53,350
And that's a relatively high-yield sort of clinical scenario that can show up on exams.

389
00:23:54,310 --> 00:23:57,610
The classic finding in a patient with carbon monoxide poisoning

390
00:23:57,610 --> 00:24:00,090
is the cherry-red appearance of the skin.

391
00:24:00,670 --> 00:24:05,890
The reason the skin looks cherry-red is because the hemoglobin is so tightly bound

392
00:24:05,890 --> 00:24:09,730
that it creates the, you know, reflects the red light

393
00:24:09,730 --> 00:24:14,150
and allows, and creates this red appearance to the skin.

394
00:24:14,490 --> 00:24:18,190
However, it's deceptive because the patient actually has,

395
00:24:19,190 --> 00:24:23,490
is actually hypoxic because they aren't carrying oxygen to their tissues.

396
00:24:23,950 --> 00:24:25,110
So although they look very red,

397
00:24:25,110 --> 00:24:27,290
they're not getting good oxygen delivery to the tissues.

398
00:24:27,990 --> 00:24:31,970
Now, another important point here, and it's kind of high-yield,

399
00:24:32,070 --> 00:24:33,910
is when you look at most textbooks,

400
00:24:34,070 --> 00:24:37,610
they'll actually list the various things that occur in stages

401
00:24:37,610 --> 00:24:39,730
as carbon monoxide poisoning occurs.

402
00:24:40,130 --> 00:24:42,190
For example, the earliest change is headache,

403
00:24:42,390 --> 00:24:46,230
and then the patients can get confusion, etc.,

404
00:24:46,230 --> 00:24:48,310
until eventually it leads to coma and death.

405
00:24:48,730 --> 00:24:51,830
Now, the leading of carbon monoxide poisoning to coma and death

406
00:24:51,830 --> 00:24:54,390
shouldn't be difficult for you to remember because, as I told you,

407
00:24:54,650 --> 00:24:56,990
it's a mechanism by which patients commit suicide

408
00:24:56,990 --> 00:24:59,070
or an individual could commit suicide.

409
00:24:59,630 --> 00:25:02,070
And so I don't think that's hard to remember.

410
00:25:02,210 --> 00:25:04,130
But what is very important to remember,

411
00:25:04,250 --> 00:25:06,130
especially for examination purposes,

412
00:25:06,690 --> 00:25:10,510
is the fact that the early sign of exposure is headache.

413
00:25:10,970 --> 00:25:13,270
And I guess this would also be important clinically

414
00:25:13,270 --> 00:25:16,990
because if you've got a patient that has been exposed to a fire

415
00:25:16,990 --> 00:25:20,850
or comes in after some type of exposure and you want to screen,

416
00:25:21,050 --> 00:25:23,730
just a very quick clinical or clinical history screen,

417
00:25:23,990 --> 00:25:25,650
you can ask the patient, do you have a headache?

418
00:25:25,730 --> 00:25:27,190
Or if the patient complains of a headache,

419
00:25:27,550 --> 00:25:29,870
you really want to then sort of raise carbon monoxide,

420
00:25:30,110 --> 00:25:31,910
the worry for carbon monoxide poisoning,

421
00:25:32,090 --> 00:25:33,170
especially with an exposure.

422
00:25:33,870 --> 00:25:35,910
So for examination purposes, I would say,

423
00:25:36,210 --> 00:25:39,550
very important to remember that an early sign of exposure is headache.

424
00:25:39,550 --> 00:25:45,710
The third way by which we could decrease the O2-carrying capacity of the blood

425
00:25:45,710 --> 00:25:47,470
is called methemoglobinemia.

426
00:25:48,170 --> 00:25:51,130
The idea here goes back to a little bit of biochemistry.

427
00:25:51,350 --> 00:25:52,730
So this is why it becomes high yield.

428
00:25:52,850 --> 00:25:56,470
Again, examiners love to take biochemical principles

429
00:25:56,470 --> 00:25:59,970
and when they apply to clinical scenarios, it's a great way to test.

430
00:26:00,510 --> 00:26:02,190
So these things become very high yield.

431
00:26:02,370 --> 00:26:04,870
You don't really see methemoglobinemia very commonly,

432
00:26:05,250 --> 00:26:06,510
except on examinations.

433
00:26:06,510 --> 00:26:12,250
So the idea here is that, remember that the heme in hemoglobin

434
00:26:12,250 --> 00:26:13,790
actually contains iron.

435
00:26:14,730 --> 00:26:18,270
And that iron is normally in an Fe2-plus state.

436
00:26:18,850 --> 00:26:21,810
Now the Fe2-plus is what binds O2.

437
00:26:22,270 --> 00:26:26,430
And I usually tell my students that this is very easy to remember.

438
00:26:26,950 --> 00:26:29,030
Fe2 binds O2.

439
00:26:29,870 --> 00:26:31,670
Fe2 binds O2.

440
00:26:31,670 --> 00:26:37,130
So if the Fe becomes oxidized and goes into a 3-plus state,

441
00:26:37,410 --> 00:26:39,710
then Fe3-plus is not able to bind O2.

442
00:26:40,390 --> 00:26:43,990
So if the iron in the hemoglobin becomes Fe3-plus,

443
00:26:44,130 --> 00:26:46,010
that is called methemoglobinemia,

444
00:26:46,390 --> 00:26:51,050
and the hemoglobin is no longer able to appropriately carry oxygen.

445
00:26:51,050 --> 00:26:54,350
Now in this particular case, the PaO2 would be normal.

446
00:26:54,490 --> 00:26:55,590
And we've already covered this.

447
00:26:55,730 --> 00:26:57,270
There's no change in the FiO2.

448
00:26:57,610 --> 00:27:01,790
There is no change in the PbAO2.

449
00:27:01,930 --> 00:27:05,270
The lung is able to fill and gather oxygen

450
00:27:05,270 --> 00:27:07,050
and blow off carbon dioxide appropriately.

451
00:27:07,630 --> 00:27:10,350
And so therefore the PaO2 in the blood,

452
00:27:10,470 --> 00:27:13,330
the partial pressure of oxygen within the blood,

453
00:27:13,410 --> 00:27:14,070
would be normal.

454
00:27:14,430 --> 00:27:16,230
The SaO2, however, is decreased

455
00:27:16,230 --> 00:27:17,850
because if you've got a red blood cell,

456
00:27:17,850 --> 00:27:20,190
then the red blood cell, again, is only able to carry,

457
00:27:20,310 --> 00:27:23,410
for example, two molecules of oxygen instead of the normal four.

458
00:27:23,790 --> 00:27:26,410
That would be a, you know, a 50% saturation.

459
00:27:27,070 --> 00:27:29,890
And so the SaO2 would be decreased.

460
00:27:30,550 --> 00:27:34,810
Now the next point to be aware of in this context is

461
00:27:34,810 --> 00:27:40,010
when and what clinical context would we actually see hemoglobinemia?

462
00:27:40,610 --> 00:27:43,870
And classically, it's seen with oxidant stresses.

463
00:27:43,870 --> 00:27:50,650
Oxidant stresses would actually have the ability to oxidize the iron.

464
00:27:50,990 --> 00:27:52,230
So what are some oxidant stresses?

465
00:27:52,450 --> 00:27:54,710
Well, sulfa drugs are oxidant stresses.

466
00:27:54,890 --> 00:27:56,950
Nitrate drugs are oxidant stresses.

467
00:27:57,290 --> 00:27:58,470
Those are the classic ones,

468
00:27:58,510 --> 00:28:00,250
and those are the way by which they tie in

469
00:28:00,250 --> 00:28:02,830
a little bit of pharmacology on examinations.

470
00:28:03,590 --> 00:28:06,050
Another group of patients that can actually develop

471
00:28:06,050 --> 00:28:07,870
methemoglobinemia are newborns.

472
00:28:08,750 --> 00:28:10,350
Now, in order to understand that,

473
00:28:10,350 --> 00:28:12,370
you should probably go back and remind yourselves

474
00:28:12,370 --> 00:28:16,530
that we're always oxidizing iron within hemoglobin,

475
00:28:16,530 --> 00:28:18,230
and all of us within our blood

476
00:28:18,230 --> 00:28:22,230
probably have a very tiny percentage of methemoglobin

477
00:28:22,230 --> 00:28:23,370
floating around in the blood.

478
00:28:23,810 --> 00:28:26,750
And that's because the blood carries oxygen,

479
00:28:26,930 --> 00:28:28,910
and there's an oxidizing stress that,

480
00:28:29,070 --> 00:28:32,090
there's an oxidant stress that's constantly present within blood.

481
00:28:32,650 --> 00:28:35,030
And so physiologically, we actually have enzymes

482
00:28:35,030 --> 00:28:39,330
that participate in creating the mediators necessary

483
00:28:39,330 --> 00:28:42,150
to reduce oxygen back to its 2-plus state.

484
00:28:42,750 --> 00:28:45,710
Sorry, reduce iron back to its 2-plus state.

485
00:28:46,310 --> 00:28:49,790
Now, newborns, their machinery to be able to do this

486
00:28:49,790 --> 00:28:50,910
is a little bit immature.

487
00:28:51,530 --> 00:28:53,790
And so newborns are particularly susceptible

488
00:28:53,790 --> 00:28:57,510
to the development of methemoglobinemia.

489
00:28:58,510 --> 00:29:01,470
The classic clinical finding in methemoglobinemia

490
00:29:01,470 --> 00:29:05,770
is chocolate-colored blood, chocolate-colored blood.

491
00:29:06,030 --> 00:29:07,970
And the patients will be cyanotic.

492
00:29:08,630 --> 00:29:12,070
So they often, you know, sort of the buzz phrases are

493
00:29:12,070 --> 00:29:14,470
cyanosis with chocolate-colored blood.

494
00:29:15,190 --> 00:29:16,850
Sorry, chocolate-colored blood.

495
00:29:17,390 --> 00:29:20,210
Now, the treatment is IV-methylene blue,

496
00:29:20,330 --> 00:29:21,810
and what does IV-methylene blue do?

497
00:29:21,990 --> 00:29:24,510
It's important for being able to generate

498
00:29:24,510 --> 00:29:27,750
the necessary mediators that will reduce iron back

499
00:29:27,750 --> 00:29:29,570
to its 2-plus state.

500
00:29:30,290 --> 00:29:32,310
And I've stated that here by saying

501
00:29:32,310 --> 00:29:34,810
that it helps reduce iron back to its 2-plus state.

502
00:29:35,330 --> 00:29:36,850
So if I were to say methemoglobinemia,

503
00:29:36,850 --> 00:29:39,690
if I wanted to summarize methemoglobinemia

504
00:29:39,690 --> 00:29:41,270
in just a very simple principle,

505
00:29:41,690 --> 00:29:46,730
I would say just remember Fe2 binds O2.

506
00:29:47,130 --> 00:29:48,910
And that's going to make it very easy to remember.

507
00:29:50,290 --> 00:29:55,170
Okay, now we get to the general theme

508
00:29:55,170 --> 00:29:58,470
of what are the consequences of hypoxia.

509
00:29:58,910 --> 00:30:00,350
So we talked about three different ways

510
00:30:00,350 --> 00:30:01,550
by which hypoxia can occur.

511
00:30:01,670 --> 00:30:04,190
For example, we talked about ischemia causing hypoxia.

512
00:30:04,530 --> 00:30:09,650
We talked about hypoxemia resulting in hypoxia.

513
00:30:09,930 --> 00:30:12,830
And then we talked about decreased O2 carrying capacity

514
00:30:12,830 --> 00:30:14,190
resulting in hypoxia.

515
00:30:14,630 --> 00:30:17,330
So when we have hypoxia or low oxygen delivery

516
00:30:17,330 --> 00:30:19,530
to the tissues, what's the consequence?

517
00:30:19,750 --> 00:30:20,890
That's sort of the next discussion.

518
00:30:21,050 --> 00:30:22,330
And the key consequence is that

519
00:30:22,330 --> 00:30:24,170
it impairs oxidative phosphorylation.

520
00:30:24,170 --> 00:30:26,990
I've already told you prior,

521
00:30:27,170 --> 00:30:28,290
and we can repeat this here,

522
00:30:28,810 --> 00:30:33,510
oxygen is necessary as the final electron acceptor

523
00:30:33,510 --> 00:30:34,990
in the electron transport chain.

524
00:30:35,610 --> 00:30:39,950
And low oxygen will result in low ability to generate ATP.

525
00:30:40,450 --> 00:30:43,870
And low ability to generate ATP will result in cell injury

526
00:30:43,870 --> 00:30:48,290
because ATP is a necessary energy source for cells.

527
00:30:48,830 --> 00:30:50,890
So the key principle here is that hypoxia

528
00:30:50,890 --> 00:30:54,690
impairs oxidative phosphorylation, which decreases ATP.

529
00:30:55,810 --> 00:30:58,370
Now, what's the problem with having a low ATP?

530
00:30:58,570 --> 00:31:00,410
And why does that cause cellular injury?

531
00:31:01,770 --> 00:31:03,470
It's particularly when it's severe.

532
00:31:04,110 --> 00:31:06,690
Well, the idea here is that ATP is necessary

533
00:31:06,690 --> 00:31:07,910
for key cellular functions.

534
00:31:08,110 --> 00:31:10,690
And perhaps the most important and highest yield

535
00:31:10,690 --> 00:31:13,290
would be the notion that ATP is necessary

536
00:31:13,290 --> 00:31:14,770
for the sodium-potassium pump.

537
00:31:15,250 --> 00:31:16,650
Now, let's go back and remind ourselves

538
00:31:16,650 --> 00:31:18,750
that cells have a sodium-potassium pump,

539
00:31:19,050 --> 00:31:20,230
and the goal of that pump

540
00:31:20,230 --> 00:31:22,210
is to push sodium outside of the cell.

541
00:31:22,690 --> 00:31:25,230
If the pump fails, in particular here,

542
00:31:25,350 --> 00:31:26,330
due to a lack of ATP,

543
00:31:26,870 --> 00:31:29,350
then sodium will build up within the cell.

544
00:31:29,930 --> 00:31:31,930
And if sodium builds up within the cell,

545
00:31:32,030 --> 00:31:32,830
guess who's going to follow?

546
00:31:33,330 --> 00:31:34,110
Yes, water.

547
00:31:34,530 --> 00:31:35,410
Water will follow,

548
00:31:35,410 --> 00:31:36,550
and when water follows,

549
00:31:36,690 --> 00:31:38,190
the cell will actually swell.

550
00:31:38,850 --> 00:31:40,910
And the swelling of the cell

551
00:31:40,910 --> 00:31:42,190
can result in damage,

552
00:31:42,190 --> 00:31:44,470
is a sign of damage to the cell.

553
00:31:44,950 --> 00:31:46,490
So this is one important function

554
00:31:46,490 --> 00:31:47,310
that gets disrupted.

555
00:31:47,470 --> 00:31:49,670
And I would say it's one of the very important functions,

556
00:31:49,770 --> 00:31:50,990
as you'll see in a moment

557
00:31:50,990 --> 00:31:52,690
when we discuss the next slide.

558
00:31:53,690 --> 00:31:58,570
The second function that gets disrupted

559
00:31:58,570 --> 00:32:01,130
is the calcium pump.

560
00:32:01,250 --> 00:32:02,410
Now, let me just tell you

561
00:32:02,410 --> 00:32:05,070
one general principle about calcium in the cell,

562
00:32:05,450 --> 00:32:08,070
and that is the goal of the cell

563
00:32:08,070 --> 00:32:11,750
is to maintain a super low concentration

564
00:32:11,750 --> 00:32:13,370
of calcium within the cytosol.

565
00:32:13,810 --> 00:32:16,750
Calcium in the cytosol is very, very low.

566
00:32:17,490 --> 00:32:19,250
Calcium can get pushed out into the blood.

567
00:32:19,410 --> 00:32:20,930
It can get hidden in the mitochondria.

568
00:32:21,070 --> 00:32:22,970
It can get hidden in the endoplasmic reticulum.

569
00:32:23,390 --> 00:32:24,870
But within the cytosol itself,

570
00:32:25,030 --> 00:32:27,110
calcium concentration is kept very low,

571
00:32:27,430 --> 00:32:28,950
and it's done using ATP,

572
00:32:29,230 --> 00:32:30,850
using an ATP-dependent pump.

573
00:32:30,850 --> 00:32:32,850
And so if there's low ATP,

574
00:32:33,250 --> 00:32:34,170
that calcium pump,

575
00:32:34,330 --> 00:32:35,590
the calcium pumps can fail,

576
00:32:36,010 --> 00:32:38,810
and that would result in a high cytosolic calcium.

577
00:32:39,170 --> 00:32:40,930
And the high cytosolic calcium is dangerous

578
00:32:40,930 --> 00:32:42,570
because it can activate enzymes,

579
00:32:42,710 --> 00:32:44,270
which you wouldn't necessarily want

580
00:32:44,270 --> 00:32:45,670
to activate within the cell.

581
00:32:46,110 --> 00:32:48,590
So calcium is an enzyme activator.

582
00:32:49,230 --> 00:32:52,650
The third disruption that occurs with low ATP

583
00:32:52,650 --> 00:32:55,850
is that we switch from aerobic glycolysis,

584
00:32:55,970 --> 00:32:58,050
which is very good at producing ATP,

585
00:32:58,050 --> 00:33:00,130
to anaerobic glycolysis,

586
00:33:00,570 --> 00:33:03,710
which then results in the byproduct of,

587
00:33:03,990 --> 00:33:05,890
number one, the poor production of ATP,

588
00:33:06,430 --> 00:33:08,750
and number two, the production of lactic acid,

589
00:33:08,870 --> 00:33:11,870
which lactic acid would lower the pH within the cell,

590
00:33:12,190 --> 00:33:14,310
and the lowering of the pH would cause precipitation

591
00:33:14,310 --> 00:33:16,610
of both DNA and proteins.

592
00:33:17,150 --> 00:33:18,470
And so this would be another problem

593
00:33:18,470 --> 00:33:22,190
that would arise when we have low ATP within the cell.

594
00:33:22,190 --> 00:33:24,590
Now, the very initial phase of injury

595
00:33:24,590 --> 00:33:27,830
that occurs when there's low ATP is reversible.

596
00:33:28,430 --> 00:33:30,590
And so examiners like you to know

597
00:33:30,590 --> 00:33:35,170
what are the various findings in reversible injury,

598
00:33:35,430 --> 00:33:36,650
and what are the various findings

599
00:33:36,650 --> 00:33:37,890
if we were to go a step further

600
00:33:37,890 --> 00:33:39,410
and have irreversible injury.

601
00:33:40,130 --> 00:33:42,330
Very, very high yield, often shows up on exam.

602
00:33:42,450 --> 00:33:44,570
So let's take a moment to actually review this.

603
00:33:45,050 --> 00:33:46,110
The initial phase of injury,

604
00:33:46,250 --> 00:33:47,570
as I state here, is reversible.

605
00:33:47,570 --> 00:33:49,310
And what I'd like you to know

606
00:33:49,310 --> 00:33:52,230
is that if you had to summarize reversible injury

607
00:33:52,230 --> 00:33:52,990
in two words,

608
00:33:53,290 --> 00:33:55,830
you would summarize it as cellular swelling.

609
00:33:56,490 --> 00:33:58,850
This is the hallmark of reversible injury.

610
00:33:59,390 --> 00:34:00,810
Now, let's just understand that.

611
00:34:00,910 --> 00:34:04,530
Again, if the sodium-potassium pump fails,

612
00:34:05,290 --> 00:34:07,910
then sodium will build up within the cell,

613
00:34:08,310 --> 00:34:10,350
and as sodium builds up within the cell,

614
00:34:10,790 --> 00:34:12,770
fluid, or sorry, water is going to follow,

615
00:34:12,950 --> 00:34:14,710
which is then going to result in swelling

616
00:34:14,710 --> 00:34:16,170
of the actual cell.

617
00:34:16,170 --> 00:34:18,310
Now, this would have consequences

618
00:34:18,310 --> 00:34:20,650
for various parts of the cell.

619
00:34:20,750 --> 00:34:23,150
For example, let's say that I have a cell

620
00:34:23,150 --> 00:34:24,150
that looks like this,

621
00:34:24,550 --> 00:34:26,210
which has these little processes

622
00:34:26,210 --> 00:34:28,290
coming off the surface called microvilli.

623
00:34:28,870 --> 00:34:30,670
Remember that microvilli are present,

624
00:34:30,790 --> 00:34:32,170
for example, in the small bowel,

625
00:34:32,410 --> 00:34:33,550
and present, for example,

626
00:34:34,030 --> 00:34:36,010
in the proximal renal tubule.

627
00:34:36,570 --> 00:34:38,170
And the purpose of these microvilli

628
00:34:38,170 --> 00:34:40,210
is to increase the surface area of the cell

629
00:34:40,210 --> 00:34:44,210
so that you could better absorb products.

630
00:34:44,210 --> 00:34:46,350
For example, in the proximal renal tubule,

631
00:34:46,470 --> 00:34:47,910
you want to absorb the things

632
00:34:47,910 --> 00:34:50,670
that you want to bring back into the blood

633
00:34:50,670 --> 00:34:52,830
from the filtrate of the glomerulus.

634
00:34:53,270 --> 00:34:54,150
In the small bowel,

635
00:34:54,270 --> 00:34:56,110
you want to be able to absorb all the food

636
00:34:56,110 --> 00:34:58,570
that's being digested along the brush border

637
00:34:58,570 --> 00:35:00,070
or the edge of the cells,

638
00:35:00,250 --> 00:35:02,050
of the enterocytes of the small bowel.

639
00:35:02,990 --> 00:35:04,130
So the idea here, again,

640
00:35:04,190 --> 00:35:06,630
is that normally some cells in the body

641
00:35:06,630 --> 00:35:07,410
have microvilli.

642
00:35:07,410 --> 00:35:11,370
However, if these cells become hypoxic

643
00:35:11,370 --> 00:35:13,010
and lose their oxygen

644
00:35:13,010 --> 00:35:15,110
and then subsequently can't produce ATP,

645
00:35:15,710 --> 00:35:17,230
sodium is going to build up in these cells.

646
00:35:17,350 --> 00:35:18,310
And as sodium builds up,

647
00:35:18,430 --> 00:35:19,350
water is going to come in.

648
00:35:19,410 --> 00:35:20,450
And as water comes in,

649
00:35:20,850 --> 00:35:22,650
these microvilli are going to be,

650
00:35:23,190 --> 00:35:24,750
the cell is going to swell up

651
00:35:24,750 --> 00:35:26,590
and these microvilli are going to be effaced

652
00:35:26,590 --> 00:35:28,550
or lost so that the microvilli

653
00:35:28,550 --> 00:35:29,550
are no longer present.

654
00:35:29,650 --> 00:35:31,410
It's sort of like taking a hospital glove

655
00:35:32,010 --> 00:35:33,130
or a medical examiner's glove

656
00:35:33,130 --> 00:35:33,930
and blowing it up

657
00:35:33,930 --> 00:35:35,430
and the fingers will disappear

658
00:35:35,430 --> 00:35:37,010
as you put more air into the glove.

659
00:35:37,430 --> 00:35:39,790
So as you put more water into the cell,

660
00:35:40,090 --> 00:35:41,750
these fingers on the surface of the cell

661
00:35:41,750 --> 00:35:42,550
are going to disappear.

662
00:35:43,130 --> 00:35:44,730
So that's why one of the hallmarks

663
00:35:44,730 --> 00:35:46,730
of reversible injury

664
00:35:46,730 --> 00:35:48,990
is loss of microvilli.

665
00:35:49,210 --> 00:35:50,850
So most textbooks will tell you

666
00:35:50,850 --> 00:35:52,550
and examinations will ask you

667
00:35:52,550 --> 00:35:54,610
about the loss of microvilli.

668
00:35:55,090 --> 00:35:56,490
Another important feature

669
00:35:56,490 --> 00:35:58,390
of reversible injury,

670
00:35:58,530 --> 00:36:00,270
it again goes back to cellular swelling,

671
00:36:00,590 --> 00:36:01,690
is membrane blubbing.

672
00:36:02,070 --> 00:36:03,590
So let's go back and remind ourselves

673
00:36:03,590 --> 00:36:04,870
that if this is the cell,

674
00:36:05,770 --> 00:36:07,170
the cell contains something

675
00:36:07,170 --> 00:36:08,430
called a cytoskeleton

676
00:36:08,430 --> 00:36:10,150
and the cytoskeleton

677
00:36:10,150 --> 00:36:12,730
maintains the size and shape of the cell

678
00:36:12,730 --> 00:36:15,650
but also maintains the integrity

679
00:36:15,650 --> 00:36:16,650
of the membrane

680
00:36:16,650 --> 00:36:18,310
along the surface of the cell.

681
00:36:18,950 --> 00:36:20,810
Now if there is excess water

682
00:36:20,810 --> 00:36:21,970
coming into the cell,

683
00:36:22,410 --> 00:36:24,310
then little blebs can form

684
00:36:24,310 --> 00:36:26,230
on the surface of the membrane

685
00:36:26,230 --> 00:36:27,630
as they sort of pull away

686
00:36:27,630 --> 00:36:28,650
from the cytoskeleton

687
00:36:28,650 --> 00:36:30,270
and that would be another sign

688
00:36:30,270 --> 00:36:31,310
of reversible injury.

689
00:36:31,450 --> 00:36:32,810
But again, what's the key principle?

690
00:36:32,810 --> 00:36:34,390
Reversible cellular swelling.

691
00:36:34,530 --> 00:36:35,390
So that's the hallmark.

692
00:36:35,470 --> 00:36:36,210
That's why I say that

693
00:36:36,210 --> 00:36:38,190
if you just understand cellular swelling,

694
00:36:38,510 --> 00:36:39,450
everything else is sort of

695
00:36:39,450 --> 00:36:40,530
a consequence of that.

696
00:36:40,990 --> 00:36:42,310
All right, the third problem

697
00:36:42,310 --> 00:36:44,410
that could arise in reversible injury

698
00:36:44,410 --> 00:36:46,510
is swelling of the rough endoplasmic reticulum.

699
00:36:46,970 --> 00:36:48,370
Now I mean if we've got a cell

700
00:36:48,370 --> 00:36:49,470
and within that cell

701
00:36:49,470 --> 00:36:51,430
we've sort of got this endoplasmic reticulum

702
00:36:51,430 --> 00:36:53,490
and this is rough endoplasmic reticulum

703
00:36:53,490 --> 00:36:57,110
with ribosomes sitting along the edge,

704
00:36:57,110 --> 00:36:59,410
that organelle itself,

705
00:36:59,510 --> 00:37:00,510
the endoplasmic reticulum

706
00:37:00,510 --> 00:37:01,650
is going to fill up with water

707
00:37:01,650 --> 00:37:02,790
and it will swell

708
00:37:02,790 --> 00:37:04,270
and when it swells

709
00:37:04,270 --> 00:37:06,130
that will also be a sign

710
00:37:06,130 --> 00:37:07,070
of reversible injury.

711
00:37:07,630 --> 00:37:08,910
Now let's go back

712
00:37:08,910 --> 00:37:09,830
and just remind ourselves

713
00:37:09,830 --> 00:37:12,070
the ribosomes that are attached

714
00:37:12,070 --> 00:37:13,770
to the endoplasmic reticulum

715
00:37:13,770 --> 00:37:16,230
are those attached covalently

716
00:37:16,230 --> 00:37:18,590
or is it a loose association?

717
00:37:19,150 --> 00:37:20,190
It's a loose association.

718
00:37:20,690 --> 00:37:22,150
And so because it's a loose association,

719
00:37:22,550 --> 00:37:23,510
one of the consequences

720
00:37:23,510 --> 00:37:25,810
of swelling of the rough endoplasmic reticulum

721
00:37:25,810 --> 00:37:27,390
is that these ribosomes

722
00:37:27,390 --> 00:37:28,610
will actually pop off.

723
00:37:29,630 --> 00:37:31,450
And so when these ribosomes pop off,

724
00:37:31,670 --> 00:37:32,570
protein can no longer

725
00:37:32,570 --> 00:37:33,790
be synthesized properly

726
00:37:33,790 --> 00:37:36,470
and therefore another sign

727
00:37:36,470 --> 00:37:38,230
of reversible injury

728
00:37:38,230 --> 00:37:40,290
is decrease in protein synthesis.

729
00:37:40,910 --> 00:37:42,130
So again, I don't think,

730
00:37:42,390 --> 00:37:42,910
you know, again,

731
00:37:43,030 --> 00:37:44,310
I don't like to memorize

732
00:37:44,310 --> 00:37:45,310
long lists of things.

733
00:37:45,410 --> 00:37:46,890
I think if you understand the principle

734
00:37:46,890 --> 00:37:47,890
then you can really

735
00:37:47,890 --> 00:37:49,570
put together the story

736
00:37:49,570 --> 00:37:50,510
and that's the way

737
00:37:50,510 --> 00:37:51,890
I would put together the story here.

738
00:37:52,290 --> 00:37:54,090
The key hallmark of reversible injury

739
00:37:54,090 --> 00:37:55,530
is cellular swelling.

740
00:37:55,530 --> 00:37:56,490
That's the take-home.

741
00:37:57,510 --> 00:37:58,590
Now, eventually,

742
00:37:58,850 --> 00:37:59,650
if there's damage

743
00:37:59,650 --> 00:38:01,770
and the damage persists

744
00:38:01,770 --> 00:38:02,930
for a long period of time,

745
00:38:03,350 --> 00:38:03,830
that damage,

746
00:38:03,950 --> 00:38:04,210
or sorry,

747
00:38:04,290 --> 00:38:04,890
if there's injury

748
00:38:04,890 --> 00:38:05,950
and that injury persists

749
00:38:05,950 --> 00:38:07,130
for a long period of time,

750
00:38:07,370 --> 00:38:08,630
that damage will eventually

751
00:38:08,630 --> 00:38:09,950
become irreversible.

752
00:38:10,530 --> 00:38:11,570
Now, if I would tell you

753
00:38:11,570 --> 00:38:13,210
that reversible injury

754
00:38:13,210 --> 00:38:15,230
is summarized by cellular swelling,

755
00:38:15,790 --> 00:38:16,470
then I would tell you

756
00:38:16,470 --> 00:38:17,750
that irreversible injury

757
00:38:17,750 --> 00:38:18,930
is summarized by something

758
00:38:18,930 --> 00:38:20,210
called membrane damage.

759
00:38:20,410 --> 00:38:21,230
That's the take-home.

760
00:38:21,590 --> 00:38:23,910
The hallmark of irreversible injury

761
00:38:23,910 --> 00:38:25,190
is membrane damage.

762
00:38:25,690 --> 00:38:26,290
We could take this

763
00:38:26,290 --> 00:38:27,070
one step further

764
00:38:27,070 --> 00:38:28,410
and we will take it

765
00:38:28,410 --> 00:38:29,110
one step further

766
00:38:29,110 --> 00:38:30,090
because it does show up

767
